# .htaccess - the headers the shipped web build needs.
#
# build.sh puts this file beside index.html. It is for Apache alone; the
# equivalent lines for nginx are in README.md.

# The three payload files carry their build's stamp in the name, and the
# on-screen pad carries a stamp of its own. What lies under such a URL never
# changes, so it may be cached for ever.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "^(blocks5-[0-9a-f]+\.(js|wasm|data)|touch_controls-[0-9a-f]+\.js)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>

  # Everything that is NOT stamped has to be asked for on every visit, and the
  # rule is that blunt on purpose: a file with no stamp and no header here gets
  # a heuristic lifetime in the browser - a fraction of its age - and goes
  # stale with nothing to say so. The pad was exactly that before it was given
  # a stamp: the page and the payload updated, the on-screen pad did not, and
  # only a private window showed the new one.
  #
  # index.html is where the stamps are written down, so caching it means nobody
  # ever sees a new build - and it is one of the two that can never carry a
  # stamp itself. sw.js is the other: it is registered under a fixed URL, and a
  # stamped one would leave the old worker alive under the old name. The icons
  # and the manifest could be stamped and are not, because they change once in
  # a few years and the manifest would have to be generated rather than copied
  # to name them.
  <FilesMatch "^(index\.html|blocks5\.html|sw\.js|manifest\.json|icon-[0-9a-z-]+\.png|apple-touch-icon\.png)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Without the right type the browser does not take the fast path
# (WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming) and loads the module the long way round.
AddType application/wasm .wasm

# mod_pagespeed has done real damage here: it kept blocks5.js under a name of
# its own (blocks5.js.pagespeed.ce.*.js) and later served that beside a newer
# blocks5.wasm. The game then aborts with "No EM_ASM constant found at address
# ...", because the EM_ASM fragments sit at addresses that fit exactly one
# wasm. The stamp in the filename makes that impossible now, but there is
# nothing here to rewrite anyway: the files come out of the build optimized.
<IfModule pagespeed_module>
  ModPagespeed off
</IfModule>
